23:45:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to question it—if that's what you were taught in multiple biology classes, it's completely understandable that you'd remember it so clearly. Let's unpack what likely happened, because you're not alone in this. ### The most likely explanation: a simple but persistent mix-up of two similar-sounding words The words **melanin** and **melatonin** are extremely easy to confuse. They share the same Greek root, *melas* (meaning "black" or "dark"), and they're both taught in biology at around the same time. Here's the difference: - **Melanin** (pronounced MEL-uh-nin) — the pigment in skin, hair, and eyes. - **Melatonin** (pronounced mel-uh-TOE-nin) — the hormone that regulates sleep, produced by the pineal gland. In many school curricula, these two terms are introduced close together (often in different chapters—skin and endocrine system), and teachers themselves sometimes slip up. A well-meaning biology teacher might accidentally say "melatonin" when they mean "melanin" dozens of times over the course of a unit, and students lock it in. Textbooks can also have typos, outdated or overly simplified language, or just ambiguous diagrams. ### Could "melatonin" have been deliberately taught as the skin pigment? It's highly unlikely that any accredited biology curriculum would intentionally teach that melatonin causes skin colour. Melatonin has never been the pigment. However, there is one nuance that *might* contribute to the confusion in some advanced or older contexts: - In some animals (like certain frogs and fish), melatonin *does* play a role in skin colour change by causing pigment granules to aggregate within cells, lightening the skin. This is an effect on the *distribution* of existing melanin, not on the pigment itself. - If a teacher was trying to stretch a connection or give a "fun fact," they might have blurred this line, leaving students with "melatonin = skin color" as a takeaway. But even then, in humans, melatonin doesn't control baseline skin tone. The pigment is unequivocally melanin. ### The real check: do you tan when you sleep? A quick way to spot the difference in practice: If melatonin determined skin colour, then taking melatonin supplements or producing more of it while you sleep would darken your skin. It doesn't. Conversely, if you stay up all night (suppressing melatonin), your skin doesn't lighten. Melanin, on the other hand, increases with sun exposure, which is the familiar tanning process. ### So how could "every biology class" get it wrong? It probably wasn't every textbook or exam question—but human memory is reconstructive. Once a strong association is formed (the word starting with "melan-" linked to skin), your brain files it away, and when you later hear "melatonin" in the context of sleep, it may feel like you learned both meanings under the same word. It's a very common memory error called *misattribution*. Many people swear they learned the same thing, only to later discover the mix-up. So you're not misremembering being told that—many of us heard it that way at some point. The important thing is that you caught the discrepancy and sought clarification, which is exactly what good science is about. Now you know the correct term is **melanin**, and you've got a great story about how easily two little letters can cause ten years of confusion!
